.TH EARTH_ORBIT 6 "2026-10-29" "1.0" "Simulation"
.SH NAME
earth_orbit \- satellite orbital mechanics simulation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B earth_orbit
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B earth_orbit
is an interactive 3\-D simulation of eight Earth\-orbiting satellites with
physically accurate Keplerian mechanics, optional J2 oblateness perturbation,
a 24\-satellite GPS Walker constellation, Mercator ground\-track map, and a
WebAssembly port for in\-browser use.
.PP
The simulation integrates orbits with a velocity\-Verlet propagator and
adaptive sub\-stepping near perigee.
Each satellite traces a fading trail; eccentric orbits (Molniya, Tundra)
colour the trail cyan at perigee and the satellite's own colour at apogee,
reflecting the speed difference.
.PP
The built\-in clock starts at J2000.0 (2000\-01\-01 12:00 UTC) and advances
at the selected time scale, displaying real calendar UTC in the status bar.
.SH SATELLITES
Eight preset orbital regimes are simulated:
.TP
.B 1 \- GEO
Geostationary orbit, equatorial, stationary above the surface.
.TP
.B 2 \- GEOSYNC
Geosynchronous orbit inclined at 28°, traces a figure\-8 analemma.
.TP
.B 3 \- MOLNIYA
Highly eccentric (e=0.74) 63.4° inclination; spends most time over high
northern latitudes.
.TP
.B 4 \- ISS
Low Earth orbit at 51.6° inclination, ~400 km altitude.
.TP
.B 5 \- POLAR
Sun\-synchronous polar orbit, 90° inclination.
.TP
.B 6 \- MEO/GPS
Medium Earth orbit, GPS altitude, 55° inclination.
.TP
.B 7 \- TUNDRA
Tundra orbit: eccentric (e=0.27) geosynchronous with 63.4° inclination.
.TP
.B 8 \- SSO
Sun\-synchronous orbit at 98.7° inclination; default slot for TLE imports.
.SH CONTROLS
.SS View
.TP
.B Left drag
Rotate the globe (3D mode).
.TP
.B Scroll wheel
Zoom in / out.
.TP
.B Click satellite
Open orbital info panel (altitude, speed, period, inclination, eccentricity).
Click again to dismiss.
.TP
.B P
Toggle between 3D sphere and Mercator map view.
.TP
.B R
Reset view to default orientation and zoom.
.SS Simulation
.TP
.B Space
Pause / resume.
.TP
.B +\~/\~=
Double the time scale (max 1,000,000×).
.TP
.B \-
Halve the time scale (min 1×, real time).
.TP
.B 1\-8
Toggle individual satellites on/off.
.TP
.B ]
Fast\-forward exactly one sidereal day (86,164 s).
.TP
.B [
Reset simulation to t=0 (J2000.0 epoch).
.SS Features
.TP
.B J
Toggle J2 zonal harmonic (Earth oblateness) perturbation.
.TP
.B G
Toggle ground\-track display (3D mode).
.TP
.B C
Toggle GPS Walker 24/6/2 constellation overlay.
.TP
.B F
Toggle satellite footprint circles.
.TP
.B H
Show / hide the key\-binding help overlay.
.TP
.B V
Start / stop MP4 video capture (requires
.BR ffmpeg (1)
in PATH; desktop build only).
.TP
.B Q\~/\~Esc
Quit.
.SS TLE import
Drop a Two\-Line Element set (.tle) file onto the canvas (WebAssembly build)
to replace satellite 8 with a real satellite's orbital parameters.
The TLE is parsed on the fly; mean motion is converted to a semi\-major axis
via the vis\-viva relation.
.SS Touch (WebAssembly)
Single\-finger drag rotates the globe; two\-finger pinch zooms.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I map_data.h
Natural Earth 1:50 m coastline and border data, generated by
.I gen_map_data.py
from the world\-atlas npm package.
.TP
.I web/index.html
WebAssembly build output.
Upload the contents of
.I web/
to any static host or serve with
.BR python3\ \-m\ http.server .
.SH BUILDING
.TP
.B make
Native binary (requires SDL2 development headers).
.TP
.B make wasm
WebAssembly build (requires Emscripten; source
.I emsdk/emsdk_env.sh
first).
.TP
.B make install
Install binary to
.I ~/.local/bin
and man page to
.IR ~/.local/share/man/man6 .
.TP
.B make zip
Create
.I earth_orbit_web.zip
for upload to itch.io.
.TP
.B make pages
Copy the WASM build into
.I docs/
for GitHub Pages deployment.
.SH PHYSICS
Orbits are propagated with a velocity\-Verlet integrator at 20 s steps,
with adaptive 1 s sub\-steps near perigee for eccentric orbits.
When J2 is enabled the \fIJ\fR\s-2\d2\u\s0 = 1.08263×10\u\s-2-3\s0\d perturbation
accelerates the satellite proportionally to the zonal harmonic of Earth's
geopotential, causing nodal regression and apsidal precession.
Orbital period is computed analytically from the semi\-major axis as
.I T = 2π√(a³/GM).
